  "query_preview": "..Aloft ridging will be in place over the next\ncouple days leading to temperatures being a few degrees above\nnormal. Highs from Tucson west will be in the low 80s and east\nof Tucson highs will be in the 70s. \n\nFriday into the weekend a longwave trough will move over the\nwestern CONUS with two embedded vort maxes. One will be on the\nsouthern end of the main trough and travel through Baja and\nSonora Friday into Saturday. The second will be further north\ntraveling through the Great Basin and the Four Corners region.\nAt the same time moisture increases with PWAT values 150-200\npercent of normal. Rain chances begin Friday afternoon and\ncontinue into Saturday with NBM PoPs generally 20-70 percent. \nThe best chances Friday will be along the international border \nsince the core of the trough will be in Mexico. Friday models\nalso show some instability (100-400 J/kg), so some isolated\nthunderstorms will be possible. Saturday will see rain chances \nmainly from Tucson north and eastward. Thunder looks less likely\nSaturday than Friday. Neither trough is positioned the best for\nheavy rainfall in southeast Arizona with mean QPF 0.01\"-0.3\" \nand localized areas up to 0.70\" in thunderstorms through \nSaturday night. Highest rainfall totals will be along the \ninternational border, mountains, and northern Graham/Greenlee \nCounties. This weather system will also bring breezy winds \nSaturday and usher in cooler temperatures with highs 6-10 \ndegrees below normal this weekend into next week.\n\nSunday looks like it will be a transition day as shortwave\nriding briefly forms before the next Pacific low moves through\nthe region. This next trough will move into California Monday\nthen Arizona Tuesday. There is some spread in the models mainly\nin the depth of the trough and how quickly it moves out Tuesday.\nHowever the amount of spread in the upper level pattern is less\nthan typically seen from this lead time, which lends some\nconfidence to the current trajectory of the trough. This low \nlooks to be on a better trajectory than the first two to deliver\nmore rainfall with the core of the low moving through Arizona \nrather than the to the north or south. Rain chances begin \nMonday evening and continue through Tuesday afternoon and \nevening. Rain totals have the potential to be higher than over \nthe weekend, but still generally 0.1\"-0.5\". Also, snow levels \nmay come down low enough for some flurries at the top of Mt \nGraham and White Mountains, but no accumulation is expected.",
